package components

// The two components left in this file are the right rail's *content*, and they
// are the route's rather than the shell's — UI §4.4 gives this slot a different
// set per route, so the landmark wraps whatever a caller passes and this is what
// the pre-campaign routes pass.
//
// `FirstRun` and `LoadError` used to live here as well. They now live in
// `components/ui` alongside the other nine of UI §4.7's states and the primitive
// library they are set in, and they left for a reason that is about the phase
// rather than about tidiness: §4.7's states are a *set*, and a set split across
// two packages is two sets with one name. A future state has no obvious home,
// and the obvious home is whichever file a reader opened first.
//
// What stayed here is what `ui` has no version of and could not have: the rail
// panels are assembled from `InstanceView`, which is a route-facing model, and
// §4.7's eleven states have no rail panel between them. So there is nothing for
// the states package to compose here, and a copy of `InstanceRail` in `ui` would
// be the second answer.

// InstanceRail is the right rail for a pre-campaign route: what this instance
// is, which build it is, and what is not working.
//
// §4.4 gives this slot to / and /login, and §4.6 says it still earns its place
// there. What it cannot show is a campaign's system, house rules or roster,
// because there is no campaign — which is why this is one panel and not five
// empty ones.
templ InstanceRail(instance InstanceView) {
	<section class="panel" aria-labelledby="instance-rail-heading" data-testid="rail-instance">
		// h2 under the centre's h1: heading levels never skip (UI §7.2), and the
		// rail follows main in the document, so this is the first subheading
		// the reader meets.
		<h2 id="instance-rail-heading">Instance</h2>
		<p class="panel-line" data-testid="rail-instance-name">{ instance.displayName() }</p>
		if instance.Version != "" {
			<p class="panel-line" data-testid="rail-instance-version">{ instance.Version }</p>
		}
	</section>
	if len(instance.Degraded) > 0 {
		@DegradedNotice(instance.Degraded)
	}
}

// DegradedNotice is the rail's standing warning that part of this instance is
// not working (UI §4.2, §4.7).
//
// Not a live region and not a toast. The condition persists until an operator
// fixes it — a watcher that has stopped watching is not an event that happened
// once — so a polite announcement would fire on every navigation for a state
// the reader can see. §4.2 puts it in the footer because the footer is the one
// part of the shell that never scrolls away; on a pre-campaign route the rail
// is where §4.4 puts it and where the reader already is.
templ DegradedNotice(degraded []DegradedView) {
	<section class="panel panel--warning" aria-labelledby="degraded-heading" data-testid="degraded-warning">
		<h2 id="degraded-heading">Not working</h2>
		<ul class="panel-list">
			for _, item := range degraded {
				<li>
					<span class="panel-term">{ item.Name }</span>
					if item.Detail != "" {
						<span class="panel-detail">{ item.Detail }</span>
					}
				</li>
			}
		</ul>
		<p class="panel-note">The rest of this instance is unaffected.</p>
	</section>
}